    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ipv6 interface all', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols ipv6 interface all\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you want to enable IPv6 on a specific interface (more precise equivalent to Cisco's `ipv6 enable` on an interface), you would use:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: The first option globally enables IPv6 on all interfaces, while the second is interface-specific like the Cisco command. Choose based on your specific needs. The interface-specific version is more directly equivalent to Cisco's per-interface `ipv6 enable`.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ipv6 interface all'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols ipv6 interface all\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you want to enable IPv6 on a specific interface (more precise equivalent to Cisco's `ipv6 enable` on an interface), you would use:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: The first option globally enables IPv6 on all interfaces, while the second is interface-specific like the Cisco command. Choose based on your specific needs. The interface-specific version is more directly equivalent to Cisco's per-interface `ipv6 enable`.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:3::1/64', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:3::1/64\\n##', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:3::1/64'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols icmpv6 interface all', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols icmpv6 interface all\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper doesn\\'t have a direct \"ipv6 enable\" command like Cisco. Instead, IPv6 is enabled by default on Juniper interfaces when IPv6 addresses are configured.\\n2. The above command enables ICMPv6 (essential for IPv6 operation) on all interfaces, which is the closest functional equivalent to Cisco\\'s \"ipv6 enable\" that activates basic IPv6 functionality.\\n3. When configuring an actual IPv6 address on a Juniper interface, you would use:\\n   `set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:3::1/64`\\n   which implicitly enables IPv6 on that interface.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols icmpv6 interface all'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces lo0 unit 1 family inet6 address 2001:db8:9::1/64', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper command for the given Cisco `ipv6 address` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces lo0 unit 1 family inet6 address 2001:db8:9::1/64\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- `lo0` is Juniper's default loopback interface (equivalent to Cisco's Loopback1)\\n- `unit 1` creates a logical unit under the interface\\n- `family inet6` specifies IPv6 address family\\n- The address and prefix length (2001:db8:9::1/64) remain unchanged\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces lo0 unit 1 family inet6 address 2001:db8:9::1/64'}]]": 1,
