    "['no synchronization', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no bgp synchronization', 'parent_command': ['set protocols bgp local-as [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp local-as [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nno bgp synchronization\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\n- In Cisco, `no synchronization` under BGP configuration disables the synchronization between BGP and IGP.\\n- The equivalent Juniper command is `no bgp synchronization`, which achieves the same functionality by disabling BGP synchronization with the IGP.\\n- The command is placed under the `routing-options` or `protocols bgp` hierarchy in Juniper, but since the question asks for the direct command translation, I've provided the core command without the hierarchy context.\", 'trans_template': 'no bgp synchronization'}]]": 1,
    "['redistribute [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols bgp group <group-name> family inet6 unicast export connected-to-bgp', 'parent_command': ['set protocols bgp local-as [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp local-as [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `redistribute connected` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols bgp group <group-name> family inet6 unicast export connected-to-bgp\\n##\\n\\nNote:\\n1. `<group-name>` should be replaced with your BGP group name (the equivalent of Cisco\\'s neighbor configuration)\\n2. You\\'ll need to define a policy called \"connected-to-bgp\" that permits the connected routes, for example:\\n```\\nset policy-options policy-statement connected-to-bgp from protocol direct\\nset policy-options policy-statement connected-to-bgp then accept\\n```\\n\\nThe exact implementation might vary slightly depending on your specific Junos version and network design.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols bgp group <group-name> family inet6 unicast export connected-to-bgp'}]]": 1,
